            title: 'Tausendeins',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tausendeins (1001, Kiautschou) is a German two-player point-trick game. First to 1001 points wins. 24 cards (9 through Ace per suit). Rank: A, 10, K, Q, J, 9. Points: A=11, 10=10, K=4, Q=3, J=2, 9=0. No trumps initially; a player on lead with King-Queen in the same suit may declare marriage (Hochzeit) by playing one and naming that suit trump. Similar to Sixty-Six; fast, free play. First published 1930 by Robert HÃ¼lsemann; likely devised by soldiers in German Kiaochow (1898â€“1914).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Various\nTime Period: Early 1900s\nHistorical Development: The game evolved from traditional card games in the region\nCultural Significance: Popular social and family game',
              },
